        <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl lg:text-4xl text-ink leading-tight mb-5">Family Therapy in Famagusta: When Should the Whole Family Come Together?</h1>
        <p class="text-ink-muted text-lg leading-relaxed">When one member of a family system is struggling, that struggle affects the whole system. Family therapy looks not at who is to blame, but at how the family functions as a unit — and works towards change from that perspective.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-12 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <div class="prose-custom space-y-8">

          <div>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Family therapy is still less well known than individual therapy in North Cyprus and the wider region, yet in many situations it produces more effective results in a shorter time. This is because the problem often lies not within a single person, but within the patterns of interaction between people.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">What is family therapy and how does it differ from individual therapy?</h2>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">In individual therapy, the psychologist works with one person — exploring their inner world, past experiences and personal patterns. In family therapy, the psychologist focuses on how family members interact with one another.</p>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Family psychologists work from a systemic perspective: rather than asking "why does this person behave this way?", they ask "why does this family system produce this pattern?" That shift changes the aims and methods of treatment fundamentally.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-primary/5 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-primary/15">
            <p class="text-sm text-ink font-medium mb-2">Family therapy does not look for someone to blame</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">The psychologist never takes sides or identifies a "bad guy". The goal is to make the communication and interaction patterns within the family visible — and to change them. Every member participates on an equal footing.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">When should a family come together for sessions?</h2>
            <div class="space-y-3">
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">Recurring conflicts and communication breakdown</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">When the same arguments cycle endlessly without resolution, or when the feeling "we can no longer talk to each other" has taken hold, family therapy helps restructure communication patterns.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">A teenager's behavioural difficulties</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">School refusal, withdrawal, aggression or risky behaviour during adolescence is often a message from the whole family system, not just from the young person alone.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">Separation or divorce</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">When parents separate, family therapy offers a structured process for deciding how to support the children, how to maintain communication, and where to draw boundaries.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">After a loss or trauma</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">The death of a family member, serious illness or a collective trauma affects every member differently. Family therapy creates a shared space for grief and healing.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="bg-warm rounded-xl p-5 border border-warm-tertiary">
                <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-1.5">A new member or major life transition</h3>
                <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">A new baby, a child leaving home, a parent returning, or a step-parent joining the family — these transitions reorganise the family's equilibrium, and adjustment can be challenging.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">Should children be included in sessions?</h2>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">This depends on the situation. Young children (0–6 years) are usually discussed in sessions attended only by the parents, with the child seen separately in play therapy. School-age children and above generally join the sessions themselves.</p>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">The psychologist decides which sessions a child attends based on their age and the nature of the issue. No child is ever pressured to take part in a session they are not ready for.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">What happens in the first family therapy session?</h2>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">In the first session, the psychologist gets to know the family members and their history. Every member is given equal space to speak, and the initial meeting is purely an assessment — no one is judged or criticised.</p>
            <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">The process typically runs for 6–12 sessions, though this varies depending on family dynamics and goals. Sessions usually take place weekly or fortnightly.</p>
          </div>

